    # Routing

    Routing is path based and inspired by
    [Next.js](https://beta.nextjs.org/docs/routing/fundamentals).

    Here's the basic structure of a potential blog application:

    * `app/`
      * `root.css`
      * `root.haml`
      * `components/`
        * `Layout/`
          * `Footer.haml`
          * `Header.haml`
          * `Layout.haml`
          * `Page.haml`
        * `UI/`
          * `Button.haml`
          * `Heading.haml`
          * `Hr.haml`
          * `Link.haml`
      * `pages/`
        * `about/`
          * `+page.haml`
        * `posts/`
          * `[id]/`
            * `+page.haml`
          * `+page.haml`
          * `+layout.haml`
        * `+page.haml`
        * `+layout.haml`

    `app/root.haml` contains the `<html>` tag, and all pages are wrapped in
    the layouts of their parent directories.

    This tree would generate the following routes:

    * `/posts/:id`
    * `/posts`
    * `/about`
    * `/`

    ## Not found

    A `+not-found.haml` file is rendered when no page matches a path. The
    closest one wins: `app/pages/posts/+not-found.haml` handles `/posts/nope`,
    and `app/pages/+not-found.haml` handles everything else.

    A page that matches but has nothing to show can raise `NotFound` while it
    renders. The closest `+not-found.haml` for the current path takes over,
    and the first request responds with status 404:

    ```haml
    :ruby
      Post = import("/models/Post")

    - post = Post.find($params[:id])
    - raise NotFound unless post

    %h1= post.title
    ```

    `NotFound` is navigation rather than an error, so error boundaries do not
    see it and nothing is logged. A `+error.haml` file handles other exceptions.

    ## Route handlers

    A `+route.rb` file handles HTTP requests for its directory. Public methods
    are named after HTTP verbs and return `[status, headers, body]`:

    ```ruby
    # app/pages/api/health/+route.rb
    def GET(_request)
      [200, {"content-type" => "application/json"}, '{"status":"ok"}']
    end
    ```

    A route can have both `+page.haml` and `+route.rb`. Browser HTML requests
    render the page; non-HTML requests are handled by the route handler.
